WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:04.163
Yesterday's day-ahead auction gave a wind farm a plan:

2
00:00:04.193 --> 00:00:07.736
deliver one hundred megawatt-hours tomorrow afternoon.

3
00:00:07.766 --> 00:00:10.317
But this morning, the forecast changed.

4
00:00:10.347 --> 00:00:12.302
Now only seventy are expected.

5
00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:14.981
Thirty megawatt-hours are missing.

6
00:00:15.011 --> 00:00:18.364
How can the wind farm fix its position before delivery?

7
00:00:18.394 --> 00:00:20.271
The answer is intraday trading.

8
00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:25.778
The day-ahead market closes well before delivery.

9
00:00:25.808 --> 00:00:29.435
After that, wind forecasts change, plants trip offline,

10
00:00:29.465 --> 00:00:31.430
and demand surprises everyone.

11
00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:36.749
The intraday market lets participants adjust their positions closer to delivery.

12
00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:39.893
It does not replace the day-ahead market.

13
00:00:39.923 --> 00:00:40.772
It refines it.

14
00:00:40.802 --> 00:00:44.851
Whatever is not corrected here will later appear as an imbalance,

15
00:00:44.881 --> 00:00:47.048
which is the topic of episode four.

16
00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:53.202
Most intraday trading is continuous, a bit like a stock market.

17
00:00:53.232 --> 00:00:56.432
Orders arrive at any time and wait in an order book.

18
00:00:56.462 --> 00:01:01.151
Sell offers are sorted from the lowest price, and buy bids from the highest.

19
00:01:01.753 --> 00:01:04.217
The best offer and the best bid sit on top.

20
00:01:04.247 --> 00:01:08.160
A trade happens when a new order reaches the other side of the book.

21
00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:11.814
And each trade is priced at the order that was already waiting.

22
00:01:14.390 --> 00:01:15.746
Back to our wind farm.

23
00:01:15.776 --> 00:01:17.698
It needs thirty megawatt-hours,

24
00:01:17.728 --> 00:01:21.352
so it sends a buy order with a limit of eighty-five euros.

25
00:01:21.382 --> 00:01:25.572
The cheapest offer is twenty megawatt-hours at seventy-eight euros,

26
00:01:25.602 --> 00:01:27.651
so twenty trade at seventy-eight.

27
00:01:28.257 --> 00:01:29.489
Ten more are needed.

28
00:01:29.519 --> 00:01:34.665
The next offer asks eighty-two euros, below the limit, so ten trade at eighty-two.

29
00:01:34.695 --> 00:01:39.209
The total cost is twenty times seventy-eight, plus ten times eighty-two:

30
00:01:39.239 --> 00:01:41.734
two thousand three hundred eighty euros,

31
00:01:41.764 --> 00:01:45.016
an average of about seventy-nine euros thirty-three.

32
00:01:45.623 --> 00:01:46.976
The position is closed.

33
00:01:47.006 --> 00:01:49.920
Notice that the two trades have different prices,

34
00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:53.406
unlike the single clearing price of the day-ahead auction.

35
00:01:53.436 --> 00:01:55.449
All numbers here are illustrative.

36
00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:00.495
Intraday orders can also match across borders.

37
00:02:00.525 --> 00:02:03.179
The single intraday coupling, known as SIDC,

38
00:02:03.209 --> 00:02:05.801
connects the order books of many countries,

39
00:02:05.831 --> 00:02:09.338
so a buyer in one zone can trade with a seller in another.

40
00:02:09.953 --> 00:02:15.085
But cross-border trades are possible only while interconnector capacity is available.

41
00:02:15.115 --> 00:02:18.425
When the capacity is used up, the order books separate,

42
00:02:18.455 --> 00:02:20.611
and prices can differ between zones.

43
00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:27.261
Since 2024, SIDC also runs three intraday auctions.

44
00:02:27.291 --> 00:02:33.648
The first closes at 15:00 on the day before delivery, the second at 22:00,

45
00:02:33.678 --> 00:02:39.948
and the third at 10:00 on the delivery day, all in central European time.

46
00:02:40.557 --> 00:02:42.124
Like the day-ahead auction,

47
00:02:42.154 --> 00:02:46.028
each one finds a single clearing price for every period it covers,

48
00:02:46.058 --> 00:02:48.868
while continuous trading carries on around them.

49
00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:56.171
In Serbia, SEEPEX launched a continuous intraday market in July twenty twenty-three,

50
00:02:56.201 --> 00:03:00.465
so participants can adjust their positions after the day-ahead auction.

51
00:03:00.495 --> 00:03:04.154
Integration with the European intraday market remains a goal,

52
00:03:04.184 --> 00:03:07.965
and timelines should be checked against official announcements.

53
00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:11.247
Three takeaways.

54
00:03:11.277 --> 00:03:11.511
One:

55
00:03:11.541 --> 00:03:16.727
intraday trading lets participants correct their positions as forecasts change.

56
00:03:16.757 --> 00:03:16.991
Two:

57
00:03:17.021 --> 00:03:19.963
in continuous trading, orders wait in a book,

58
00:03:19.993 --> 00:03:23.000
and each trade is priced at the waiting order.

59
00:03:23.030 --> 00:03:23.396
Three:

60
00:03:23.426 --> 00:03:26.830
cross-border matching depends on available capacity.

61
00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:31.256
Next time, we look at balancing markets and imbalance settlement.

62
00:03:31.286 --> 00:03:33.278
Thanks for watching Power Academy.
